{"seconds": 65.3, "exit": 0, "final": "White is up the queen! So after Rd8, whether Black plays Qxd8 or Nxh5, White wins decisive material. This looks like THE move.\n\nLet me verify that Rd8 is the clearly best move by checking if there's anything better. The move Qxh6+ doesn't seem to lead to a forced win like Rd8 does. Let me make sure Rd8 is indeed the best first move and get the full line.\n\nThe key line is: **1. Rd8** and then:\n- If 1...Qxd8 2. Qxd8+ Rf8 (or Kh7 3. Bxf7) 3. Qxf8+ winning\n- If 1...Nxh5 2. Rxf8+ winning the queen\n\nThis is a back rank mate threat combined with winning the queen - a devastating double threat.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Rd8\", \"line\": [\"Rd8\", \"Qxd8\", \"Qxd8+\", \"Rf8\", \"Qxf8+\"], \"motifs\": [\"backRankMate\", \"fork\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching Note:** Your rook on d8 threatens the queen on the back rank while your bishop controls f7, leaving Black in zugzwang. Black's king has no escape squares (h8 is trapped by its own pawns). The rook move forces the queen to defend, but after Qxd8 Qxd8+ the back rank collapses. This is a classic back rank weakness\u2014look for quiet rook moves to the back rank when your opponent's king has limited mobility.", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "5q1k/5rp1/pQ5p/2p4B/5nP1/8/P6P/3R3K w - - 1 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "5q1k/5rp1/pQ5p/2p4B/5nP1/8/P6P/3R3K w - - 1 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "5q1k/5rp1/pQ5p/2p4B/5nP1/8/P6P/3R3K w - - 1 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "5q1k/5rp1/pQ5p/2p4B/5nP1/8/P6P/3R3K w - - 1 37", "move": "Qxh6+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "5q1k/5rp1/pQ5p/2p4B/5nP1/8/P6P/3R3K w - - 1 37", "move": "Rd8"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "5q1k/5rp1/pQ5p/2p4B/5nP1/8/P6P/3R3K w - - 1 37", "move": "Qg6"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "5q1k/5rp1/pQ5p